<h2>What changed, and why</h2>

<p>The Commercial Courts Act was conceived as an ecosystem: specialised courts, compressed timelines, strict pleading and disclosure standards — and a costs regime with consequences. The Act's Schedule rewrote Section 35 CPC for commercial disputes on the model of the English rule, and the 2018 amendments added pre-institution mediation under Section 12A to push parties toward resolution before a plaint is ever filed. Costs are the enforcement mechanism of the whole design: timelines bite because wasting them is expensive; disclosure obligations bite because suppression invites adverse costs; frivolous claims and defences bite their makers.</p>

<h2>The structure of the substituted Section 35</h2>

<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><h3>Discretion, defined</h3><p>The court decides whether costs are payable, their amount, and when they are to be paid — with "costs" defined to cover witness expenses, legal fees and other litigation expenditure.</p></div>
  <div class="fstep"><h3>The general rule</h3><p>Costs follow the event: the loser pays. A different order requires reasons recorded in writing.</p></div>
  <div class="fstep"><h3>The balancing factors</h3><p>Conduct of the parties; success on part of the case even if not wholly successful; frivolous claims or counterclaims; and reasonable settlement offers unreasonably refused.</p></div>
  <div class="fstep"><h3>The menu of orders</h3><p>Costs may be awarded as a proportion, a stated amount, from or until a specified date, or for particular steps in the proceedings — allowing surgical, stage-specific allocations.</p></div>
</div>

<h2>Old world and new world</h2>

<div class="compare">
  <div class="col old"><h3>Ordinary civil suit</h3><p>Costs in the court's discretion, in practice nominal; compensatory costs for false or vexatious claims capped at modest statutory figures; delay rarely priced.</p></div>
  <div class="arrow">→</div>
  <div class="col new"><h3>Commercial suit</h3><p>Realistic costs follow the event; written reasons needed to depart; conduct, partial success and refused offers expressly weighed; costs can attach to specific stages and defaults.</p></div>
</div>

<h2>Litigation habits the regime rewards</h2>

<div class="check">
  <p>Maintain a contemporaneous costs record from day one — fee agreements, invoices, witness expenses — so the bill of costs is provable, not reconstructed.</p>
  <p>Make settlement offers in writing, early, and keep them open for a defined period; an unreasonably refused offer is the cheapest insurance against a long trial.</p>
  <p>Plead and disclose with discipline: struck-out pleas, false denials of documents and abandoned claims each carry a costs tail.</p>
  <p>Price delay before seeking it: adjournments and failed interlocutory skirmishes in commercial courts increasingly arrive with costs attached.</p>
</div>

<p>The regime also reframes advice to clients at the filing stage. A commercial claim worth pursuing must be worth its defended cost — and a defence maintained only to buy time can now add the opponent's legal fees to the judgment debt. Conversely, a successful party who litigated economically and offered settlement sensibly can expect the costs order to reflect it. The honest answer to "what will this case cost" in a commercial court includes both sides' bills in the losing scenario, and that answer, delivered early, settles more disputes than any procedural reform.</p>

<div class="note"><p>This is a general explainer on the costs framework applicable to commercial disputes under the Commercial Courts Act, 2015. Costs orders remain discretionary and fact-sensitive, and practice varies between courts; nothing here is legal advice.</p></div>
